WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:08.320
Glass, to me, is crazy cool.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.150
I make a collection of very 
bespoke and unique glass objects

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.800
that have their own aesthetic.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.280
I went to high school in Bermuda, and from an early age,

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:21.400
I was lucky enough to have a kiln.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.280
What fascinates me most, is how you can create

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.960
something organically
with limited resources.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:33.040
All you need is mud
and a flame to ignite it,

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.320
then voilà, you've got a sculpture
that will last forever.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:42.200
In the past, I've looked at woodwork,
metalwork and digital processes,

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:46.600
but eventually, I chose glass as my main medium.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.040
When I discovered glass-blowing
and glass-sculpting,

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.120
I was immediately fascinated

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.880
with how a material could be manipulated
in so many different ways.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.600
Mold-blowing
is one of the oldest techniques.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:01.920
In my own practice

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.800
I make dozens of pieces
from one digital mold,

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.200
which is really sustainable,

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.240
as there are no by-products or waste.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.680
My work begins with sketches
and drawings,

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.520
which then evolve
onto a digital platform,

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.480
to carry out a computerised edit.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.120
From that point,

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:31.840
I moved on to 3D-sculpting
and 3D-cutting

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.640
to form objects from the sketches.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.960
When I started working
in a more professional capacity,

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.520
I became aware 
of the limitations of glass-making

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.880
and how inaccessible it was
in certain places around the world.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.880
The cultural imprint
behind glass-making,

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:51.600
exists even today.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.480
I'm want to inspire
young people

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.000
from all backgrounds and ethnicities.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.400
It's really important
for people to be aware

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.960
that if an occupation
doesn't seem viable to them,

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.360
because of the colour of their skin 
or their personality,

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:08.240
they shouldn't give up.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.560
If you have a passion for something,
you should pursue it.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:21.080
Sometimes you have
to change the status quo.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.000
When I'm working with glass,

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:29.280
there's a constant battle
between how I think and how I feel.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:35.040
There's a sense of suspense and drama,

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.080
because if you don't concentrate
on what you're doing,

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:39.800
it could all go terribly wrong.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:46.200
It's a constant battle with weight, sweat, heat and fire.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:51.440
One of the roles that traditional
methods play in my own work

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.400
is continuity.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:57.240
In my work
there's a real sense of history,

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:00.640
and I think it's important to acknowledge

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.360
the culture and heritage of glass-making.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.560
It's great to be able to amalgamate
digital-based concepts and designs

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.280
with more traditional methods

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.800
to create an original, contemporary style.

